data.content:
Here's the review. Three real problems, plus assessment of the two skipped tests.

---

## Problem 1 — `Audit_RunsWithoutError`: Assertion-Free Test

**Severity: High**
This test has zero assertions. It exercises `Audit()` but verifies nothing — it passes even if `Audit()` silently corrupts state. A test without assertions is a false green light; it inflates pass counts and breeds false confidence. That makes it high severity.

**Corrected code:**
```csharp
[Fact]
public void Audit_RunsWithoutError()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    service.Audit();

    // Assert the observable side-effect of auditing, e.g.:
    Assert.True(service.LastAuditCompleted);
    // If Audit() truly has no observable outcome, question
    // whether this test should exist at all.
}
```
If there's no observable side-effect, at minimum wrap it in `var ex = Record.Exception(() => service.Audit()); Assert.Null(ex);` — but the better fix is to assert actual behavior.

---

## Problem 2 — `Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes`: Sleepy Test (`Thread.Sleep`)

**Severity: High**
A hard 3-second sleep makes the test slow *and* flaky — it will spuriously fail on a loaded CI agent if 3 s isn't enough, and wastes time when the operation is fast. This is high because flaky tests erode trust in the entire suite and slow every CI run.

**Corrected code:**
```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    await service.ReplenishAsync("SKU-1");

    Assert.True(service.WasReplenished("SKU-1"));
}
```
If `ReplenishAsync` returns `void` (fire-and-forget), refactor it to return `Task`. If that's not possible, poll with a timeout:
```csharp
var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
while (!service.WasReplenished("SKU-1") && DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    await Task.Delay(50);
Assert.True(service.WasReplenished("SKU-1"));
```

---

## Problem 3 — `Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities`: Bare Skip with no reason

**Severity: Medium**
The skip text is just `"skip"` — no tracking issue, no rationale. This is medium because the test itself is well-structured (clear arrange/act/assert), so the code isn't dangerous, but an unexplained skip silently hides dead coverage. It will rot and nobody will know when it's safe to re-enable.

**Corrected code:**
```csharp
[Fact(Skip = "Tracked by #XXXX - <describe the blocking reason>")]
public void Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities()
{
    // ... body unchanged ...
}
```
Better yet: remove the skip and fix whatever blocks it. If the test is sound, ship it enabled.

---

## Assessment of the Two Disabled Tests

| Test | Worry level | Why |
|------|-------------|-----|
| `Reserve_AcrossWarehouses_BalancesStock` | **Low concern.** The skip message names a ticket (`#1487`) and a concrete blocker (warehouse API redesign). Someone can find and track it. Periodically verify the ticket is still open. | |
| `Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities` | **Medium concern.** `"skip"` tells you nothing. There's no way to know if this was disabled yesterday or two years ago, whether the underlying issue is tracked, or when to re-enable it. Treat it as a silent coverage gap until someone adds context or re-enables it. | |

---

**Summary:** Two high-severity issues (assertion-free test, `Thread.Sleep`) that actively harm suite reliability, one medium-severity housekeeping issue (bare skip), and one well-documented skip that just needs periodic triage. The first two tests (`AddItems`, `RemoveItem`) are clean — the inline literals are contextually obvious and don't warrant extraction.
